Final serious suggestion: Look into sous vide. It is a little expensive to get into, but you literally get a big pot of water, stick your immersion cooker into it, set the temperature, and throw your protein (and even your veggies) into a ziplock bag or vacuum seal bag and force most/all the air out and then let it sit in the hot water for an hour or two. Timing isn't as important with sous vide, so if you need to sit there for another half an hour to get yourself built up to finish dinner, it's waiting for you no problem. You just have to finish off the protein usually with a quick sear, 60 seconds a side in a hot pan. I also do a semi-complicated sous vide sweet potato that involves cooking for an hour at like 150 to maximize sweetness, then I crank it up to 180 for an hour to soften the sweet potatoes. I do asparagus in like 10 minutes, there's other veg you can do too. It's a cool cooking method.

